| `lab_sparksql_and_dataframes.ipynb` | PySpark DataFrames and Spark SQL, exploratory data analysis | NYC TLC yellow taxi trip records (Jan 2019, Jan 2025, full year 2019) |

Both notebooks download their own source data on first run, so no data files are
committed to the repository.

---

## 1. `word_count.ipynb` — RDD fundamentals

Introduction to the RDD API through a classic word count on the English text of
*Around the World in Eighty Days*.

**Word count pipeline.** An RDD is built from the raw text file, lines are split
into words with `flatMap`, and occurrences are counted with `map` +
`reduceByKey`. The notebook contrasts a transformation (which only records the
operation and returns an RDD reference, thanks to Spark's lazy evaluation) with
an action such as `collect()`, which actually triggers the computation and pulls
the results back to the driver.

The pipeline is then refined one step at a time:

- lower-casing all words
- filtering out a stop word list
- sorting alphabetically with `sortByKey`
- sorting by descending frequency with `sortBy`
- stripping punctuation and empty tokens

The final cell chains every transformation into a single reusable
`process_text(rdd, stop_words)` function, as required by the instructions.

**Reading an existing pipeline.** The second part comments, line by line, a
`map` / `reduceByKey` / `map` chain that computes the average age per name from
a parallelized list of tuples.

---

## 2. `lab_sparksql_and_dataframes.ipynb` — DataFrames and Spark SQL

Exploratory data analysis on the
[NYC TLC Trip Record Data](https://www.nyc.gov/site/tlc/about/tlc-trip-record-data.page).
PySpark is used as the main language for Parts 1 and 2; Part 3 re-answers three
of the questions in pure Spark SQL.

### Part 1 — Trip-level analysis (PySpark)

A unique `trip_id` is added with `monotonically_increasing_id()`, then the
January 2019 dataset (~7.7M trips) is explored: passenger counts, shortest and
longest trips by distance and by duration, busiest and slowest days, hourly and
time-of-day buckets, day-of-week averages, the relationship between trip
distance / passenger count and tip amount, the highest `extra` charge, and an
outlier detection pass.

Selected findings:

- Average passenger count: **1.57**
- Busiest single day: **25 January 2019** (~292k trips); the afternoon
  (12:00–17:00) is the busiest time-of-day bucket, and 18:00 the busiest hour
- Busiest day of the week on average: **Thursday** (~194k trips/day), slowest:
  **Monday** (~91k)
- Trip distance correlates moderately with tip amount (**r ≈ 0.53**), while
  passenger count essentially does not (**r ≈ 0.004**). Average tip rises from
  $0.94 on trips under 1 mile to $6.22 on trips over 10 miles

### Part 2 — Borough-level analysis (PySpark)

The taxi zone lookup table is joined on `PULocationID` and `DOLocationID` to
attach a borough to every pickup and dropoff, then aggregated by borough:
pickups and dropoffs, busy and slow hours, busiest days of the week, average
trip distance, average fare, and the highest and lowest fares with their
associated borough. Finally, January 2025 is loaded and compared against
January 2019.

Selected findings:

- **Manhattan** dominates both pickups (~6.95M) and dropoffs (~6.82M), far
  ahead of Queens
- Average trip distance is *inversely* related to density: Staten Island
  (12.5 mi) and Queens (11.3 mi) lead, Manhattan is last (2.2 mi) — short
  intra-borough hops in the core, long outbound trips from the periphery.
  Average fares follow the same pattern, with EWR (Newark Airport) highest at
  ~$76
- 2019 vs 2025: average distance more than doubled (2.83 → 5.86 mi), average
  fare rose from $12.53 to $17.08, average tip from $1.82 to $2.96, while the
  average passenger count fell from 1.57 to 1.30

### Part 3 — Spark SQL

Three questions re-answered in pure SQL via temporary views (`trips`, `zones`):
average passenger count, highest `extra` charge, and pickups per borough — the
latter using a `LEFT JOIN` on the zone lookup table, as required.

### Outliers

The dataset contains a substantial amount of dirty data, documented in the
notebook: dropoff timestamps at or before the pickup, trips of 831 miles,
durations of 30 days, fares up to \$623,259, negative fares and negative `extra`
charges (refunds recorded as trips), and `passenger_count` values of 0 or 9 —
above the legal capacity of a yellow cab. Pickup dates also fall outside
January 2019 in a handful of records. These would be filtered or handled
separately in a production pipeline rather than dropped blindly.

### Where to go from here

The notebook also loads the full year 2019 (~85M trips) to compute the busiest
season — **spring** (22.9M trips) ahead of winter, fall and summer — and
includes native Spark 4 DataFrame plots plus ideas for cross-referencing with
other open datasets.
